Eastward I stand,     for honors I pray;
I pray to the God of glory;     I pray to the gracious Lord;
I pray to the high and holy     Heavenly Father; …

Loud were they, lo loud,     as over the lea they rode;
Resolute they were     when they rode over the land.
Protect thyself that thy trouble     become cured and healed. …

Bethlehem was named the town where Christ was born;
It is renowned through all the world;
So may this deed become famed among men …

(Un)clothed Himself God almighty when He would
mount the cross, courageous in the sight of all men;
bow . . . . …

Living I was, I spake not aught,
yet, nevertheless, I kill ;
before I was, again I came ; …

Month of Janus, the coom is smoke-fuming;
Weary the wine-bearer; minstrels far roaming;
Lean are the kine; the bees never humming; …

Month of January--smoky is the vale;
Weary the wine-bearer; strolling the minstrel;
Lean the cow; seldom the hum of the bee; …

I AM Taliesin the ardent;
I will enrich the praise of baptism.
At the baptism of the ruler, the worshipper wondered, …

In the name of the Ruler of the high powers of heaven,
They sing of, they deplore the prince;
He rejected uniform ranks of the rulers, …

The lion will be most implacable;
I will not deplore him.
Urien I will approach, …

In manliness he will greet my trouble,
Should I be bled, I should evidently get better;
Truly I saw. no one before, who saw not in me …

The Awen foretells the hastening of
The multitude, possessed of wealth and peace;
And a bountiful sovereign, and eloquent princes. …

A BARD there is here, who has not sung, what he shall have to sing;
Lot him sing; when he shall have finished,
An astrologer then he may be. …

A PLEASANT virtue, extreme, penance to an extreme course;
Also pleasant, when God is delivering me.
Pleasant, the carousal that hinders not mental exertion; …

THE head Bard's place I hold
To Elphin, chieftain bold;
The country of my birth …

I have come to thee to tell
Of the jurisdiction I have in the North;
The beauty of every region has been described to me. …

O little pig! thou happy little pig!
Bury not thy snout on the top of the mountain;
Burrow in a secluded place in the woods, …

Usual is wind from the south; usual is noise;
In the village usual for the weakling to be slender;
Usual for a man to inquire after news. …

The Calends of winter, hard is the grain;
The leaves are on the move, the plash is full;
In the morning before he sets off, …

Entangling is the snare, clustered is the ash;
The ducks are in the pond; white breaks the wave;
More powerful than a hundred is the counsel of the heart. …

Bright are the ash-tops; tall and white will they be
When they grow in the upper part of the dingle;
The languid heart, longing is her complaint. …

Set is the snare, the ash clusters glow,
Ducks plash in the pools; breakers whiten below;
More strong than a hundred is the heart's hidden woe. …

Horseman resorts to the city,
With his white dogs, and large horns;
I, who have not before seen thee, know thee not. …

Seithenhin, stand thou forth,
And behold the billowy rows;
The sea has covered the plain of Gwydneu. …

The three depredatory horses of the Isle of Prydain:--
Carnawlawg, the horse of Owain the son of Urien;
Bucheslwm Seri, the horse of Gwgawn Gleddyvrudd; …

Keen is the gale, bare the hill,
It is difficult to find a shelter;
The ford is turbid, frozen is the lake, …

On a fine night Pen Gethin heard the shout of a host,
When he took a long leap;
Unless the ground be guarded he will not cease. …

Soon it will happen that kindred by nature will be in the shout of war,
Soon will happen many a cut from the tournament;
Soon will come between Saxons a recoil …

CHRIST JESUS! who art in complete possession of light,
The strength of the feeble Christian in the gloom;
Christ, the mysterious One! in order to produce seriousness …

Now gone are my ardour and liveliness;
If I have erred, I truly acknowledge it;
May the Lord not inflict upon me severe pain! …

In the name of the Lord, mine to adore, whose praise is great.
I will praise the great Ruler, whose blessing is great on an alms-deed;
The God that defends us, the God that made us, the God that will deliver us, …

As long as we sojourn among excess and pride,
Let our work be perfect;
Let us seek deliverance through faith, …

Mountain snow--every region is white;
The raven is accustomed to sing.
No good will come from long sleeping. …

Long is the day without Usnagh's Children;
It was never mournful to be in their company.
A king's sons, by whom exiles were rewarded, …

White shields they carry in their hands,
With emblems of pale silver;
With glittering blue swords, …

I am Eve, great Adam's wife,
'Tis I that outraged Jesus of old;
'Tis I that robbed my children of Heaven, …

Summer has come, healthy and free,
Whence the brown wood is aslope;
The slender nimble deer leap, …

Four men stood by the grave of a man,
The grave of Alexander the Proud;
They sang words without falsehood …

A hedge of trees surrounds me,
A blackbird's lay sings to me;
Above my lined booklet …

Adam's lifetime was not short;
that ye may know, without risk (of error),
thirty years had he, it was exactly proven, …

O Ness, let all men stand,
The hour of thy peril is at hand;
Pale daughter of old Eochad Buidhe the mild …

Arise, O Champion of Ulster!
In joyous health mayest thou awake;
Look thou on Macha's King, beloved, …

It is I who must go on this journey,
Ou great necessity were best for me;
Though another should have an equal fame …

Splendid in your eyes may be the impetuous champions
